      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by expressing both sides of the equation with the same base. However, it's not straightforward to do so directly, so we will take the common logarithm (or log base 10) of both sides to eliminate the exponents:\n\n\\[\n\\log(7^{x+7}) = \\log(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\log(a^b) = b \\log(a)\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n(x + 7) \\log(7) = x \\log(8)\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand \\((x + 7) \\log(7)\\):\n\n\\[\nx \\log(7) + 7 \\log(7) = x \\log(8)\n\\]\n\nTo isolate the variable \\(x\\), we move \\(x \\log(7)\\) to the left side:\n\n\\[\n7 \\log(7) = x \\log(8) - x \\log(7)\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the right side:\n\n\\[\n7 \\log(7) = x (\\log(8) - \\log(7))\n\\]\n\nNow, solve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\log(8) - \\log(7)\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\log(7)}{\\log(8) - \\log(7)}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite the denominator using the property of logarithms \\(\\log(a) - \\log(b) = \\log\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\log(7)}{\\log\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nThis expression matches the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). To match the bases of the logarithms, we note that:\n\n\\[\n\\log_b 7^7 = \\log_b (7^7) = 7 \\log_b 7\n\\]\n\nHowever, since the original expression had a base of 10 in the logarithms and the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\) suggests a different base, we identify that the base \\(b\\) must be such that \\(\\log_b 7 = 1\\) (which implies \\(b = 7\\)) when \\(x = \\log_7 7\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\log_7 7\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(b\\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
