      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\) for \\(x\\), we will use logarithms to isolate \\(x\\). Here are the steps:\n\n1. **Take the logarithm of both sides:**\n   \\[\n   \\log(7^{x+7}) = \\log(8^x)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Apply the power rule of logarithms \\(\\log(a^b) = b \\log(a)\\):**\n   \\[\n   (x+7) \\log 7 = x \\log 8\n   \\]\n\n3. **Distribute the logarithms:**\n   \\[\n   x \\log 7 + 7 \\log 7 = x \\log 8\n   \\]\n\n4. **Isolate \\(x\\) on one side of the equation:**\n   \\[\n   x \\log 8 - x \\log 7 = -7 \\log 7\n   \\]\n\n5. **Factor out \\(x\\) on the left-hand side:**\n   \\[\n   x (\\log 8 - \\log 7) = -7 \\log 7\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solve for \\(x\\):**\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-7 \\log 7}{\\log 8 - \\log 7}\n   \\]\n\n   Notice that the denominator \\(\\log 8 - \\log 7\\) can be simplified using the property of logarithms \\(\\log(a) - \\log(b) = \\log\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\log 8 - \\log 7 = \\log \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-7 \\log 7}{\\log \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Express \\(x\\) in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\):**\n   \\[\n   x = \\log_b 7^7\n   \\]\n\n   From the equation \\(x = \\frac{-7 \\log 7}{\\log \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)}\\), we see that the value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\log_b 7^7\\). Comparing this with the general form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\), we can directly identify that \\(b\\) is the base of the logarithm in the numerator \\(\\log 7\\).\n\n8. **Identify the base \\(b\\):**\n   \\[\n   b = 7\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(b\\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
